.TH ATLAS-AWK 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-awk \- Treat each line as fields and run a tiny program: print columns, filter, count, add up.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
awk  \-F:  '$3 >= 1000 { print $1, $6 }'  users.txt
 |     |    |   |           |            '\-\-\- input file
 |     |    |   |           '\-\-\- action: print field 1 and field 6
 |     |    |   '\-\-\- condition (pattern): field 3 is at least 1000
 |     |    '\-\-\- the program is in single quotes
 |     '\-\-\- \-F sets the field separator (default: whitespace)
 '\-\-\- the program
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Pull a column out of a log, \fB/etc/passwd\fR, \fBps\fR or a CSV.
.IP \(bu 2
Count events per IP or user (a one\-line report).
.IP \(bu 2
Sum or average numbers; reformat output.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-F SEP\fR
Field separator (\fB\-F:\fR, \fB\-F,\fR, \fB\-F'\et'\fR).
.br
Use it when: Colon, comma or tab separated data.
.TP
\fB$1 $2 ... $NF\fR
Field 1, 2 ... the last field.
.br
Use it when: Pick columns.
.TP
\fB$0\fR
The whole line.
.br
Use it when: Print or test everything.
.TP
\fBNR / NF\fR
Line number / number of fields.
.br
Use it when: Skip headers (\fBNR>1\fR), check shape.
.TP
\fBBEGIN { } / END { }\fR
Run once before / after all lines.
.br
Use it when: Totals and reports.
.TP
\fBarr[key]++\fR
Associative arrays.
.br
Use it when: Counting per key.
.TP
\fBprintf\fR
Formatted output.
.br
Use it when: Aligned columns.
.TP
\fB\-v var=value\fR
Pass a shell value into awk.
.br
Use it when: Parameterise a filter.
.TP
\fB~ / !~\fR
Regex match / no match.
.br
Use it when: Test a field against a pattern.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Print columns"
Fields are split on whitespace: 1 = IP, 7 = path, 9 = status in this log format.
.PP
.nf
$ awk '{print $1, $7, $9}' access.log | head \-4
.fi
.PP
.nf
10.0.0.5 /index.html 200
10.0.0.5 /login 200
10.0.0.9 /login 401
10.0.0.9 /login 401
.fi
.PP
IP, path, status for each request. Counting fields on one line first (\fBawk '{print NF}' | head \-1\fR) avoids off\-by\-one mistakes.
.SS "2. Filter on a field"
The part before \fB{...}\fR is a condition. Only matching lines run the action.
.PP
.nf
$ awk '$9 == 401 {print $1, $7}' access.log
.fi
.PP
.nf
10.0.0.9 /login
10.0.0.9 /login
10.0.0.9 /login
.fi
.PP
Only the three failed logins appear.
.SS "3. Count per IP (associative array)"
\fBc[$1]++\fR counts per IP. \fBEND\fR prints the table after all lines are read.
.PP
.nf
$ awk '{c[$1]++} END {for (ip in c) print c[ip], ip}' access.log | sort \-rn
.fi
.PP
.nf
5 10.0.0.9
2 10.0.0.7
2 10.0.0.5
.fi
.PP
Requests per IP, busiest first. Same shape as the grep pipeline but you can add conditions.
.SS "4. Sum a column"
\fB$NF\fR is the last field (the byte count). \fBNR\fR at the end is the number of lines read.
.PP
.nf
$ awk '{s += $NF} END {print s, "bytes in", NR, "requests"}' access.log
.fi
.PP
.nf
4222 bytes in 9 requests
.fi
.PP
Total bytes served and the request count.
.SS "5. Use another separator"
\fB\-F:\fR splits on colons, as in \fB/etc/passwd\fR. Field 3 is the UID, field 6 the home directory.
.PP
.nf
$ awk \-F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1, "\->", $6}' users.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
alice \-> /home/alice
bob \-> /home/bob
.fi
.PP
Normal users (UID 1000 and up) with their home folders. \fBsvc\-backup\fR (UID 998) is skipped.
.SS "6. Format a small report"
\fBBEGIN\fR runs before the first line; \fBprintf\fR pads columns.
.PP
.nf
$ awk \-F: 'BEGIN {printf "%\-12s %\-6s\en", "USER", "UID"} {printf "%\-12s %\-6s\en", $1, $3}' users.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
USER         UID   
root         0     
alice        1001  
bob          1002  
svc\-backup   998   
.fi
.PP
A neat two\-column table. Handy for screenshots in a writeup.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Single\-quote the program; double quotes let the shell expand \fB$1\fR first.
.IP \(bu 2
Fields are 1\-based; \fB$0\fR is the whole line.
.IP \(bu 2
Quoted strings inside awk use double quotes; escape them inside \fBbash \-c\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
awk is not one program: gawk (used for these examples), mawk (the default on some Ubuntu installs) and BSD awk differ in features such as \fBasort\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR and regex intervals.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Print the unique status codes in \fBaccess.log\fR with counts.
.IP 2. 3
Print lines where the byte count is greater than 1000.
.IP 3. 3
Print each username in \fBusers.txt\fR in upper case (\fBtoupper\fR).
.SH SEE ALSO
cut, sort, uniq, sed, column \-t, python \-c.
.SH CAPTURE
Output captured 2026\-11\-23 with: GNU Awk 5.2.1, API 3.2, PMA Avon 8\-g1, (GNU MPFR 4.2.1, GNU MP 6.3.0)
